\section{Composition Requires a Contract}

Markus Voelter and collaborators in \textit{DSL Engineering} discuss modular language design and integration. A practical extension point should state which parser and lexer mode own it, which tree category it may return, how siblings are ordered, and how an embedding ends. Where independent modules claim the same start tokens, check the combined grammar rather than trusting individual unit tests. Prefer rejecting a collision or requiring an explicit priority rule to silently depending on module load order.

Test an extension against a corpus of base programs and compare their normalized trees with and without it. Document intentional differences, such as the new tree for \texttt{show all}, alongside protected cases such as \texttt{show alloy} and the bare name \texttt{all}. Then test the module in combination with another module using the same hook, reversing installation order if order is meant to be irrelevant. Finally, keep recognition and binding contracts separate: the successful production determines shape, not necessarily the declaration to which a reference points. Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-138} develops the symbol interface, while Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-137} considers what happens when an embedded construct is only half written.

