\section{Installing Statement Extensions at a Token Gate}

Not every extension needs to compete in a prioritized choice. A host
can reserve a marker for third-party statements, then assign each
installed module a unique keyword after the marker. Consider a
statement form beginning with \texttt{@} and an identifier. The host
validates a registry of descriptors before parsing input. Each
descriptor names a keyword, a module, and a parser for a delimited
payload. The result of validation is a dispatch table or a collision
report. Assume case-sensitive keywords, and a host scanner that can
identify the end of the payload without invoking any plugin. The parser
then returns a tagged statement or a diagnostic confined to that form.
Unmarked statements still go to the host grammar.

\begin{sudocode}
  \Input validated registry $D$, next statement tokens $T$
  \Output host node, extension node, or diagnostic
  \If{$\Call{Peek}{T}\ne\texttt{@}$}
    \State \Return $\Call{HostStatement}{T}$
  \EndIf
  \State $q\gets\Call{ReadKeyword}{T}$ or report syntax error
  \State $P\gets\Call{TakePayload}{T}$ or report boundary error
  \Comment{Host owns the closing token}
  \If{$q\notin D$}
    \State \Return $\Call{UnknownExtension}{q}$
  \EndIf
  \State $r\gets\Call{Parse}{D[q],P}$
  \If{$r$ failed or did not consume all of $P$}
    \State \Return $\Call{PayloadError}{q,r}$
    \Comment{Do not try a rival rule}
  \EndIf
  \State \Return $\Call{ExtensionNode}{q,r.node}$
\end{sudocode}

Install a math module under \texttt{sum} and a logging module under
\texttt{note}. On \texttt{@sum[2,3]}, the payload scanner supplies
the tokens \texttt{2 , 3} without the brackets. The math parser
consumes three tokens and returns the integer pair $(2,3)$, which
the host tags as a \texttt{sum} node. If the math parser consumes
only \texttt{2}, the consumed length is $1$ rather than $3$ and
the statement is rejected. If a second descriptor claims
\texttt{sum}, reject the registry before reading any source and
name both owners. Reversing module order must produce the same
conflict, not a different meaning for the example input.

Once the host passes the token gate, selection is a single lookup and
independent of which other payload grammars are installed. That
invariant protects the meaning of existing marked forms, but it
does not establish that a plugin recognizes a deterministic language
internally. The delimiter contract is also substantive: if nested
brackets or quoted closing tokens are legal, the host scanner must
account for them. A missing close is a host error, not an invitation
for another plugin to claim the remaining input.
